<%# The standing band: where you stand this period, in one sentence.
    Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter). See the UI design spec §4.1. %>
<div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded p-6">
  <% if presenter.covered? %>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">You're covered this period</h2>
    <%# "after this period" is not padding. The categories band below prints an `available now`
        figure too — what is unclaimed at this instant — and one screen carrying the same word over
        two different moments leaves the reader to guess which is which, or worse, to read one as a
        breakdown of the other. %>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
      <%= number_to_currency(presenter.projected_buffer) %> is still unclaimed after this period.
    </p>

    <%# THE ORPHAN CLAUSE IS GONE (Task 6). It said "$X of what you need belongs to N pools with no
        account, listed below — being covered can't fix that". A category belongs to no account by
        design, so there is no such money and no such band. %>
  <% else %>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-status-danger">
      <%= number_to_currency(presenter.shortfall) %> short this period
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
      You need <strong><%= number_to_currency(presenter.total_required) %></strong> to stay on schedule.
      You have <strong><%= number_to_currency(presenter.available) %></strong>.
    </p>

    <%# THE ONE REASON THE TWO FIGURES ABOVE NO LONGER SUBTRACT TO THE HEADLINE. There used to be
        two — cash stranded in an account whose own pools were funded, and money owed by a pool no
        account could reach — and both died with the accounts: one root, so a short period drains it
        to zero and `total_required - available` IS the shortfall.

        What survives is available going BELOW zero, which is money already promised out of the
        root. A distribution cannot be short by more than everything it was asked for, so the
        headline stops at `total_required` and this clause carries the rest. Silent otherwise, since
        a consequence is only worth stating when there is one (principle 3). %>
    <% if presenter.available.negative? %>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600" data-available-in-the-red>
        Available is <%= number_to_currency(presenter.available) %> — more has been spent or claimed
        than came in, so the gap above is what your categories miss, not what you owe.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# WHICH PERIOD THE BAND ABOVE IS TALKING ABOUT. Read off HomePresenter#period_range, itself
      User#period_containing — the one window every other screen trusts, never re-derived here.
      Silent for an undeclared user (`period_range` is nil): the band already renders without a
      period in that state, and inventing a calendar-month boundary nobody set would be a lie beside
      a sentence that is otherwise still true. %>
  <% if presenter.period_range %>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500" data-period-range>
      <%= presenter.period_range.first.strftime("%b %-d") %> – <%= presenter.period_range.last.strftime("%b %-d") %>
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <%# AN OVERDRAWN ACCOUNT IS A FACT ABOUT THE PHYSICAL LEDGER and neither figure above counts it:
      the purpose ledger's root is what `available` is, and money already spent out of a bank
      account is not in it.

      NO ROW VOCABULARY HERE ANY MORE (Task 6). This printed `pool_status_label(status_for(account))`
      — an account went through PoolStatus, which read its balance and called it `overdrawn $412.00`.
      A status is a reading of what a HOLDER holds against the rules filling it, and an account holds
      no rules; the figure is the whole of what there is to say, so the sentence says it directly. %>
  <% presenter.overdrawn_accounts.each do |account| %>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded bg-status-danger-light px-3 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-status-danger"
       data-overdrawn-account="<%= account.name %>">
      <%= account.name %> is overdrawn <%= number_to_currency(presenter.overdraft_for(account)) %> — already
      spent, and none of the figures above count it.
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <%# §9: A PERMANENTLY VISIBLE BUTTON, AND ONLY WHEN INCOME < RULES. The same destination the
      Budget page's structural check points at, because there is one answer to "what do I cut" and
      it is one screen.

      `structurally_underwater?` is gated on income AND cadence together and memoised for the false
      case too, so this caller costs nothing. A recomputation here could say "your budget doesn't fit
      your income" beside a /budget page that says it fits — and the route this button opens refuses
      on exactly that reader's condition.

      This band's own headline is about THIS PERIOD'S CASH and can say "You're covered" while this
      button is showing: they are different questions, which is why §9 asks for the button to be
      permanent rather than conditional on the shortfall. %>
  <% if presenter.structurally_underwater? %>
    <%= link_to("Your budget doesn't fit your income",
                sacrifice_path,
                class: "mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded border " \
                       "border-status-danger text-status-danger text-sm font-medium hover:bg-white",
                data: { sacrifice_link: true }) %>
  <% end %>
</div>
